{"name":"Nectar of the Gods","hex":"#513439","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nectar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The drink of the gods. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any delicious drink, now especially a type of sweetened fruit juice."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To feed on nectar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nectar"},{"term":"gods","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of god"]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Ellipsis of oh gods."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gods"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Banisters","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Banisters is the eighth studio album by American singer-songwriter Lana Del Rey. It was released on October 22, 2021, by Interscope and Polydor Records, seven months after her previous record, Chemtrails over the Country Club. The album was produced by Del Rey, Zachary Dawes, Drew Erickson, Loren Humphrey, Mike Dean, Barrie-James O'Neill, Rick Nowels, and several others."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/10/Lana_Del_Rey_-_Blue_Banisters_cover.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Banisters"},{"title":"Ambrosia","blocks":[{"text":"In the ancient Greek myths, ambrosia is the food or drink of the Greek gods, and is often depicted as conferring longevity or immortality upon whoever consumed it. It was brought to the gods in Olympus by doves and served either by Hebe or by Ganymede at the heavenly feast."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/WLANL_-_MicheleLovesArt_-_Museum_Boijmans_Van_Beuningen_-_Istoriato_schotel%2C_de_maaltijd_der_Goden_op_de_Olympus.jpg/330px-WLANL_-_MicheleLovesArt_-_Museum_Boijmans_Van_Beuningen_-_Istoriato_schotel%2C_de_maaltijd_der_Goden_op_de_Olympus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambrosia"},{"title":"Nectar","blocks":[{"text":"Nectar is a viscous, sugar-rich liquid produced by plants in glands called nectaries, either within the flowers with which it attracts pollinating animals, or by extrafloral nectaries, which provide a nutrient source to animal mutualists, which in turn provide herbivore protection. Common nectar-consuming pollinators include mosquitoes, hoverflies, wasps, bees, butterflies and moths, hummingbirds, honeyeaters and bats. \nNectar is an economically important substance as it is the sugar source for honey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Nectar.jpg/330px-Nectar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nectar"},{"title":"Deity","blocks":[{"text":"A deity is a supernatural being considered to have authority over some aspect of the universe and/or life, with many also being considered as sacred and worthy of worship. It is \"a spirit or being believed to have created, or to control some part of the universe or life, for which such a deity is often worshipped\". The Oxford Dictionary of English explains deity as being anything revered as divine; while C. Scott Littleton defines a deity as \"a being with powers greater than those of ordinary humans, but who interacts with humans, positively or negatively, in ways that carry humans to new levels of consciousness, beyond the grounded preoccupations of ordinary life\". A male deity is called a god, while a female deity is called a goddess."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Maler_der_Grabkammer_der_Nefertari_001.jpg/330px-Maler_der_Grabkammer_der_Nefertari_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deity"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Johann_Balthasar_Probst_002.jpg/330px-Johann_Balthasar_Probst_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Johann_Balthasar_Probst_002.jpg/330px-Johann_Balthasar_Probst_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Thetis anoints Achilles with ambrosia, by Johann Balthasar Probst (1673–1748)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohann_Balthasar_Probst_002.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Mosaik_%22Wahnsinn_des_Lykurg%22_%E2%80%94_Exponat_in_der_Arch%C3%A4ologischen_Staatssammlung_M%C3%BCnchen.JPG/500px-Mosaik_%22Wahnsinn_des_Lykurg%22_%E2%80%94_Exponat_in_der_Arch%C3%A4ologischen_Staatssammlung_M%C3%BCnchen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Mosaik_%22Wahnsinn_des_Lykurg%22_%E2%80%94_Exponat_in_der_Arch%C3%A4ologischen_Staatssammlung_M%C3%BCnchen.JPG/500px-Mosaik_%22Wahnsinn_des_Lykurg%22_%E2%80%94_Exponat_in_der_Arch%C3%A4ologischen_Staatssammlung_M%C3%BCnchen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lycurgus attacking the nymph Ambrosia (mosaic from Herculaneum, 45–79 AD)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMosaik_%22Wahnsinn_des_Lykurg%22_%E2%80%94_Exponat_in_der_Arch%C3%A4ologischen_Staatssammlung_M%C3%BCnchen.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg/500px-Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg/1280px-Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg/500px-Buckwheat_flowers_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt